4. RESULTS 

4.1 Structural characterization of MexB conformational states 

The three-dimensional structure of the MexB multidrug efflux transporter from Pseudomonas aeruginosa was analyzed using the biological assembly of PDB structure 3W9I. The MexB homotrimer consists of three protomers representing distinct functional conformations. Chain A was assigned as the Access state, chain B as the Binding state, and chain C as the Extrusion state. 

To compare the conformations while minimizing the effect of overall protomer orientation, the transmembrane domains (TMDs) were used for structural superposition. A total of 246 TMD atoms were used for each protomer. The Binding state showed an RMSD of 1.838 Å relative to the Access state, whereas the Extrusion state showed an RMSD of 1.347 Å relative to the Access state. These values indicate that the transmembrane regions remain relatively conserved between the conformational states (Murakami et al., 2006). 

Changes in the porter domain were assessed using PN1–PN2 and PC1–PC2 distances. The PN1–PN2 distance increased from 24.95 Å in the Access state to 26.25 Å in the Binding state and 28.29 Å in the Extrusion state. In contrast, the PC1–PC2 distance increased from 23.36 Å in Access to 25.46 Å in Binding and subsequently decreased to 21.43 Å in Extrusion. These measurements demonstrate distinct conformational rearrangements within the porter domain during the MexB transport cycle. 

