4.8 Summary of major findings 

The structural analysis of MexB revealed measurable conformational differences between the Access, Binding, and Extrusion states, particularly within the porter domain, while the TMD regions remained comparatively conserved. 

P2Rank analysis identified a ligand-binding region in the Binding-state structure, which was selected as the Deep Binding Pocket. Six residues VAL139, PHE178, TYR327, PHE573, PHE610, and PHE628 were defined as the core DBP residues. 

Docking of 30 candidate compounds resulted in five computationally prioritized ligands with predicted Vina scores ranging from −10.44 to −12.90 kcal/mol. Importantly, all five compounds demonstrated contacts with all six DBP core residues within 4 Å. 

The representative top-ranked compound, CHEMBL254328, showed a Vina score of −12.90 kcal/mol and established close contacts with all six core residues. Three-dimensional visualization confirmed its localization within the predicted DBP. 

Docking of four experimentally characterized reference ligands resulted in 4/6 to 6/6 core-residue contacts, with three of four reference ligands showing at least five core contacts. Collectively, these findings support the use of the selected Binding-state DBP as a structurally relevant site for computational ligand screening. 

The identified compounds should be regarded as computationally prioritized DBP-binding candidates. Their predicted docking scores and structural contacts do not constitute experimental evidence of MexB inhibition, and further experimental or molecular-dynamics-based validation would be required. 

 
